"In the chapter on attention in The Principles of Psychology, William James wrote two sentences that read like a description of mindfulness. My experience is what I agree to attend to. Only those items which I notice shape my mind. And later: The faculty of voluntarily bringing back a wandering attention, over and over again, is the very root of judgment, character, and will. No one is compos sui if he have it not. Compos sui means master of oneself. Then he made an admission. An education which should improve this faculty would be the education par excellence. But it is easier to define this ideal than to give practical directions for bringing it about. The Buddhist tradition had spent two thousand years on exactly those practical directions. The basic instruction in breathing meditation is what James describes: notice that the mind has wandered, and bring it back, over and over again.": "27fae1df3610",
"Looking after yourself, you look after others; looking after others, you look after yourself.": "edc3abd7e134",
"Most Westerners have met mindfulness already, through an app, a workplace course, or a therapist. Do not start by correcting them. Start with the bowl of oil, which shows how alert and how calm real attention is. Then read William James sentence about bringing back a wandering attention, and point out that he wanted exactly the training that meditation provides. Mention Epictetus to show that the West once had its own practice of attention. Then make the two further points. Watching closely enough shows that the watcher is also a process. And the acrobats show that attention is meant to include care for others. Close with the basic instruction, because the audience can try it before they leave: notice the breath, notice when the mind wanders, and bring it back.": "a052a16eab08",
"The Buddha compared it to carrying a full bowl of oil through a crowd without spilling a drop.": "3e2970aa7dfe",
"The French historian Pierre Hadot argued that ancient philosophy was not mainly a set of theories. It was a way of life, sustained by what he called spiritual exercises: attention to the present moment, the examination of conscience, the view from above, the rehearsal of misfortune, the memorizing of key maxims to have them at hand. In his reading, attention to oneself was the fundamental Stoic attitude, and the doctrines served the practice and were not its goal. Hadot noticed the parallel with Buddhist meditation and wrote about it with interest and caution. His work matters here for a different reason. It shows that the Western tradition once had a practice of attention as serious as the Buddhist one, and that modern academic philosophy forgot it. A Westerner who takes up mindfulness is in one sense recovering something from their own past, and Hadot is the best guide to what that was.": "d4c7d217a3d1",
"The Pali word for mindfulness, sati, originally meant memory: keeping something in mind.": "a259f63a6300",
"The Pali word sati first meant memory, and the suttas keep that sense. Defining the faculty of mindfulness, the Buddha says that a noble disciple can remember and recall what was said and done long ago (Saṁyutta Nikāya 48.10, in Bhikkhu Sujatos translation). Mindfulness is a kind of remembering that keeps the present object in mind and does not let it slip. The Discourse on Mindfulness Meditation opens with large claims. The Middle-Length Discourses in Chinese render it: There is one path that purifies beings, carries them past grief and fear, ends pain and distress, stops weeping, and attains the true Dharma: the four foundations of mindfulness. The four are body, feelings, mind, and phenomena. In each the practitioner is, in Sujatos rendering of the Pali, keen, aware, and mindful, rid of covetousness and displeasure for the world.": "b535e721bf68",
"The Stoics had a word for attention, prosochē, and Epictetus devoted a discourse to it. In George Longs translation: When you have remitted your attention for a short time, do not imagine this, that you will recover it when you choose. A lapse forms a habit of not attending and then a habit of deferring your attention. He asks, Is there any part of life excepted, to which attention does not extend? and answers with the carpenter and the ships captain, who do not work better by not attending. Like sati, prosochē is practiced in ordinary activity: Today I choose to play. Well then, ought you not to play with attention? What is attended to is a little different. Epictetus wants us to watch our impressions and ask, How shall I use the appearances presented to me? according to nature, or contrary to nature? The aim is to keep the will on what is in its power and to let go of the rest.": "7038aef27e62",
"The Stoics practiced attention too, to keep reason in charge. Buddhists practice it until they see that no one is in charge.": "d2a615bd2ea0",
"The closest is Epictetus. Prosochē and sati are both disciplines of constant attention, both are practiced in ordinary activity, and both aim at the gap between an event and our response to it. Hadot showed that this attention was the center of Stoic practice, as sati is the center of Buddhist practice. William James is closest on the psychology. He saw that the power to return a wandering mind is the root of character and that training it would be the highest education, and he could not say how to train it.": "b90da4067dfb",
"The first further step is to watch the watcher. Epictetus, James, and Hadot all assume someone who attends, a reason, a will, or a self in formation, and the practice strengthens that someone. The fourth foundation of mindfulness turns attention on the elements of experience themselves, including the mind that is attending. The meditator who notices that the mind has wandered can also notice that the noticing arises and passes. This is the point at which mindfulness becomes insight. Nothing in the Western practices of attention requires it, and most Western philosophers would not know what to do with it. It is also where a teacher should be most careful, because the claim can only be checked in practice, the way the means of knowledge lesson described.": "2d4a8ec9e503",
"The likeness ends at what attention is for. The Stoic watches in order to judge rightly. The ruling reason inspects each impression and assents only to what is true and in its power, and the self that does this is strengthened by the practice. The Buddhist watches body, feeling, mind, and phenomena until they are seen to arise and pass, and the watcher is included. The practice does not strengthen a ruler. It shows that there was never one. The Stoic ideal is a mind in command of itself, and the Buddhist ideal is a mind no longer driven by craving, which is a different thing. The practices can look the same for years, and they lead in different directions.": "70c94caa716b",
"The practice is ordinary. When walking they know: I am walking. When standing they know: I am standing. The Chinese text has the same: When walking, know walking; when standing, know standing; when sitting, know sitting; when lying down, know lying down. The practitioner brings the same plain knowing to feelings as they arise, pleasant, painful, or neutral, to states of mind such as anger or calm, and to the patterns of experience the Buddha taught, such as the hindrances and the factors of awakening. Nothing here requires a special place. The instructions include eating, drinking, dressing, and going to the toilet.": "a0bed1b4b5f8",
"The second step is the acrobats. Modern secular mindfulness programs have brought sati to millions of people, and much good has come of it. They usually present it as a technique for reducing stress, with the ethics removed, and critics inside and outside Buddhism have asked whether a soldier or a trader can use it to do harm more calmly. The suttas answer in advance. Mindfulness is right mindfulness only within the path, alongside right intention and right action. And the acrobat sutta says that looking after yourself is looking after others, and looking after others means acceptance, harmlessness, love, and sympathy. A teacher presenting mindfulness to Westerners can honor the secular programs and still say what they leave out. In the original teaching, attention is for liberation, and it includes care for others.": "605a3c085785",
"Try it now: notice the breath, notice when the mind wanders, and bring it back.": "3b3bc7009f51",
"Two short suttas give the spirit of it. In one, a man is told to carry a bowl full to the brim with oil between a large crowd and the finest lady in the land, who is dancing and singing, while a man with a drawn sword will follow right behind you. Wherever you spill even a drop, hell chop off your head right there. Would he lose focus on that bowl? No. A bowl of oil filled to the brim is a term for mindfulness of the body (Saṁyutta Nikāya 47.20). In the other, two acrobats on a bamboo pole discuss safety. The teacher says, You look after me, and I shall look after you. The apprentice disagrees: You look after yourself, and I shall look after myself. The Buddha sides with the apprentice and then adds, Looking after yourself, you look after others; and looking after others, you look after yourself, the latter by acceptance, harmlessness, love, and sympathy (Saṁyutta Nikāya 47.19). Mindfulness looks inward, and the suttas never treat it as private.": "64d2d714b260",
"William James said that bringing back a wandering attention is the very root of judgment, character, and will.": "8b230ad32ce0"
}
